    "['term [parameter2] then [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map ldp-export permit 20', 'parent_command': ['policy-map [parameter1]'], 'root': 'policy-map [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map ldp-export permit 20\\nmatch ip address prefix-list 2.2.2.9/32\\n##\\n  \\nNote: In Cisco IOS:\\n1. \"route-map\" replaces \"policy-statement\"\\n2. \"permit\" replaces \"accept\"\\n3. The term number \"2\" becomes sequence number \"20\" (typically Cisco uses multiples of 10)\\n4. You would need a separate prefix-list configuration like \"ip prefix-list PL_2.2.2.9 seq 5 permit 2.2.2.9/32\" to match the exact route', 'trans_template': 'route-map ldp-export permit 20'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match ip address prefix-list 2.2.2.9/32', 'parent_command': ['policy-map [parameter1]'], 'root': 'policy-map [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map ldp-export permit 20\\nmatch ip address prefix-list 2.2.2.9/32\\n##\\n  \\nNote: In Cisco IOS:\\n1. \"route-map\" replaces \"policy-statement\"\\n2. \"permit\" replaces \"accept\"\\n3. The term number \"2\" becomes sequence number \"20\" (typically Cisco uses multiples of 10)\\n4. You would need a separate prefix-list configuration like \"ip prefix-list PL_2.2.2.9 seq 5 permit 2.2.2.9/32\" to match the exact route', 'trans_template': 'match ip address prefix-list 2.2.2.9/32'}]]": 1,
